Diagnosed token regeneration issue across multiple file includes
Diagnosed token regeneration issue across multiple file includes

This is almost always caused by the token being regenerated every time the layout/header partial is included, rather than being generated once per session.

Here's the classic bug pattern:

php
// header.php or layout.php (included by every page)
session_start();
$_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32)); // ❌ regenerates every include!

If your layout system does something like:

php
<?php include 'layouts/header.php'; ?>
<?php yield('content'); ?>
<?php include 'layouts/footer.php'; ?>

...and header.php is pulled in by every page (or by every one of those "3 more php files"), the token gets overwritten each time a new file is loaded. So the hidden <input> in your form has an old token value, but by the time the form is submitted, $_SESSION['csrf_token'] has already been replaced by a later include — validation fails because they no longer match.

The fix — only generate it if it doesn't already exist:

php
session_start();
if (empty($_SESSION['csrf_token'])) {
    $_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));
}

That single if (empty(...)) check is 90% of the fix for this exact symptom.

Other things to check, especially with multi-file/AJAX-loaded pages (common with SB Admin 2 layouts):

session_start() called more than once, or after output — if any of those 3 extra PHP files call session_start() again without checking session_status(), you can get warnings/behavior issues:
php
if (session_status() === PHP_SESSION_NONE) {
    session_start();
}
Each AJAX-loaded content page generating its own token — if SB Admin 2's sidebar loads pages via AJAX into a content div, and each of those loaded PHP files independently regenerates the token (thinking it's a "fresh page load"), the form on screen and the session token drift apart. Only generate the token in one central bootstrap file, included first, before anything else runs.
Multiple forms per page each calling a "new token" function — if you have a reusable form component that calls something like csrf_field() and that function creates a new token every call, having 3+ files/forms on one page will cause only the last one to actually match the session.
Caching — if any of those 3 php files or the layout is being cached (opcache aside, browser cache or a full-page cache), you can serve a stale token in the HTML while the session moves on.

Quick way to confirm which of these it is: temporarily var_dump($_SESSION['csrf_token']) right before your validation check, and also right where you print the hidden input — if they don't match, add a debug_print_backtrace() or just a error_log() line inside your token-generation code so you can see how many times it's firing per request.

If you paste the actual snippet where you generate the token and where the layout/yield/include structure lives, I can point at the exact line causing it.

